course: dictionary
lesson: 15576
title: Swampy places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Swampy describes land that is wet, soft and muddy, like a swamp. Water lies on the ground, and plants grow in it. When you walk on swampy ground, your feet can sink.
explain: «Our shoes sank into the swampy field after the rain.» The clues are sank and after the rain. Something that sinks is soft, and rain makes ground wet, so swampy must mean wet and soft.
explain: Swampy is an adjective, because it describes a noun such as ground, path or field. It can change to compare places: «The park is swampy, but the bog is swampier, and the marsh is swampiest of all.»
fact: A swamp is a wetland, a place where the ground is covered with water for much of the year.
fact: Swamps and marshes are homes for frogs, birds and many insects.
fact: In the USA, alligators live in swamps.
fact: Wetlands store water, and this helps to stop floods.
fact: Swampy places are often used in stories as spooky, misty places, such as Shrek's swamp.
exercise: «The swampy ground squelched under our boots.» What does swampy mean? | *Wet, soft and muddy | Covered in thick snow | Hard and dry like a road | Hot and sandy
exercise: Which animals might you find living in a swamp? | Camels and penguins | Polar bears on ice | *Frogs and birds | Sharks in a desert
exercise: Copy the word that means wet and muddy: «The swampy field was full of puddles.» | field | puddles | full | *swampy
test: «Our shoes sank into the swampy path.» What does sank tell you about the path? | It is very high up | *It is soft and wet | It is made of stone | It is hard and dry
test: Which word compares two wet places, the second being wetter than the first? | swampy-est | swampily | *swampier | swampiest
exam: Why do writers often put a witch or a monster in a swampy place? | To show the monster likes sunbathing | To show the witch is rich | To make the story end quickly | *To make the place feel spooky and damp
